 "notes": [
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>The takomeshi and the sweet-soy baby octopus are both Okayama regional recipes as published by Japan's Ministry of Agriculture, Forestry and Fisheries; the cleaning follows Honda's fishing guide to iidako.</li><li>Takomeshi is commonly eaten as onigiri.</li><li><b>This site's version:</b> baby octopus instead of octopus legs, half the pieces cooked in the rice and half glazed then folded in (nothing on top), reducing the sauce to a glaze, the triangle and the nori strip, and the amounts for six.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "What's established and what's this site's"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Iidako means “rice octopus”: in winter the heads are full of roe that looks like grains of cooked rice — a delicacy in Okayama.</li><li>If yours have it, you can leave it in: snip off the eyes and beak and pull out only the innards below the roe.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "“Rice octopus”"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Low heat for the first simmer, and never a hard boil.</li><li>The Okayama recipe also beats the tentacles lightly (with a piece of daikon) to soften them — worth doing if yours are on the large side.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Tender, not rubbery"
  },
  {
   "html": "<p>Use <span class=\"q\" data-k=\"mass\" data-u=\"g\" data-a=\"200\" data-b=\"300\" data-s=\"0\">200–300 g</span> of octopus legs, cut into <span class=\"q\" data-k=\"len\" data-u=\"cm\" data-a=\"1\" data-s=\"0\">1 cm</span> pieces: half in the rice cooker, half glazed the same way.</p>",
   "title_html": "No baby octopus?"
  },
  {
   "html": "<ul class=\"do\"><li>Best within a few hours, at cool room temperature.</li><li>Cold rice goes hard — don't refrigerate the shaped onigiri.</li></ul>",
   "title_html": "Keeping them"
  }
 ],
